Yes. Because in order to make use of an operation system functionality, you
need a userland program to invoke that functionality. Be that a single- or
few-purpose executable or a shell. The creators of touch explicitly had
this function in mind. Touch is often used to create empty files, mostly
to tinker with processes that check the existence of certain files but
don't care about the contents. For that purpose, it is extremely
convenient compared to other methods.

